  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082348/assets/a988c243bcf4b82cd05b1ed0292e0595141dbe37a470abc34b183f0ce184fe3f.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2 \\\\ y+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8} \\\\ (y+2)^{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =(y+2)^{3}-8 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{-2}^{0}\\,+\\,6\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{0}+12\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{16}{4})+6(0-(-\\frac{8}{3}))+12(0-\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =-4+16-24 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-12|=12\\,.\n$$",
